# A tour of the .http format: variables, queries, bodies, headers, filters.
#
#   api run examples/users.http --list            what is in the file
#   api run examples/users.http "List users"      one request, by name
#   api run examples/users.http 2 .data[0]        by number, with a jq filter
#   api run examples/users.http --all --dry       print every request, send none
#   api run examples/users.http "Get user" --var id=7
#
# A path that starts with / goes to the ACTIVE server (api env use, --env or
# --base) and carries your session token. Change the paths to match your API.
# A full URL on another host gets no token unless you pass --trust-hosts.

# File variables: "@name = value", used as {{name}}. --var name=value beats them.
@page = 1
@limit = 10
@id = 42

### List users
# @filter .data
GET /users?page={{page}}&limit={{limit}}
Accept: application/json

### Search users
# A long query can be split over indented ? and & lines.
GET /users/search
  ?q=ann
  &limit={{limit}}

### Get user
GET /users/{{id}}

### Create user
# {{$uuid}}, {{$timestamp}} and {{$isoTimestamp}} are filled in at send time.
POST /users
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$uuid}}

{
  "name": "Ann",
  "email": "ann@example.test",
  "createdAt": "{{$isoTimestamp}}"
}

### Create user from a file
# "< file" reads the body from a file, relative to this .http file.
POST /users
Content-Type: application/json

< ./user.json

### Delete user
DELETE /users/{{id}}
